.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2261:deliverables below. Return a per-axis table + weighted overall + PASS/FAIL
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2264:## Lane K scope (8 sub-phases)
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2266:K.1 cargo-llvm-cov CI:
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2267:  .github/workflows/coverage.yml (soft 80%/70% v1.3 gate; baseline-diff
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2270:K.2 cargo-deny + cargo-audit + cargo-msrv:
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2273:K.3 #![forbid(unsafe_code)] on every crate root + bin entry + SAFETY-doc:
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2277:K.4 proptest expansion (3+3+3+2 = 11 properties across 4 crates, 256 cases):
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2284:K.5 cargo-fuzz 3 targets:
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2289:K.6 cross-platform matrix (4 jobs):
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2293:K.7 criterion benches (5 hot paths):
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2300:K.8 SBOM + cosign + SLSA L3:
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2311:- workspace tests: 781 baseline → 790 with K.4 additions (+9 tests pass)
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2315:## Rubric
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2317:7 axes from `.agent/active/v1_3_quality_bar.md`. Lane K D-axis specialization:
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2318:"coverage 80%+ / fuzz nightly / cross-platform 4 matrix / SBOM/sigstore."
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2332:overall = X.XX (weighted)
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2337:/bin/bash -lc "sed -n '1,220p' .agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out" in $REPO_ROOT
.agent/active/scoring/v1_3_k_score_round1_codex.out:2351:I’m moving from the reviewer summaries into the actual Lane K surfaces now: workflows, `unsafe` guards, property tests, fuzz targets, and benches. The key distinction for scoring is whether a sub-phase has only a configured gate or also has durable, deterministic evidence that the gate works.
